Z||000004||Leper||09-30-2001||03:05 AM||||I always thought that car was sweet.<BR>Maybe someday I can build one similar.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Chris McDonald||09-30-2001||08:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leper:<BR><B>I always thought that car was sweet.<BR>Maybe someday I can build one similar.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is why I sent this link.  Maybe it will help inspire others.  When you see what I had to work with from the start, it should make you feel a whole lot better about your project.<P>The original aluminum headed motor was over kill-I was not aware of anybody that could make a clutch strong enough to harness that kind of power.  The other six or so missing pictures detail the second engine install.  I had also switched to a five speed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSoul||09-30-2001||09:04 AM||||Chris,<BR>  Is that an 84 or one of the newer years.  I am asking because I have an 84 and keep hearing about all the wiring problems with upgrading to a 6 or 8. Any stories?  <BR>Thanks, Dwayne||||reg||6
Z||000007||Chris McDonald||09-30-2001||09:11 AM||||Yes!!!!!!!!!!! I just found the other pictures that have been M.I.A. for so long now.  Click on this link and go to the very end to see the final engine swap.  You will notice the second engine had cast iron heads and I painted it black.  <P>This car started life out as an 84 but got body panels from an 86.  I did not have any wiring problems that I can recall.  <P>If you look close at the second engine swap, you will see the F-body a/c compressor.  <BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=1428880&a=13584211" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=1428880&a=13584211</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||KissMySSFiero||09-30-2001||10:39 AM||||i have to say that is the best put together car i have ever seen.  after seeing the car at twin lakes in may,  all i can say is pictures do no justice.  <P>Chris,  when you had the hot engine what tranny did you use?  4.10 4spd or was it a 4spd from another car?  also how much power do you think that engine put out?<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Chris McDonald||09-30-2001||01:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B><BR>Chris,  when you had the hot engine what tranny did you use?  4.10 4spd or was it a 4spd from another car?  also how much power do you think that engine put out?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the compliment.  I used both the Muncie 4 speed (stock fiero 4 cyl trans), and two different five speed (Isuzu).<P>I would desk top dyno the "hot" engine between 375 and 425 hsp.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowfunds||09-30-2001||04:29 PM||||Wow! Beautiful job, its nice to see such time and effort go into a swap. Again great job.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mafv8||09-30-2001||05:08 PM||||Chris, I bought your Fiero from Jeff Lorenz back in July and I have been enjoying it ever since, when I bought it I drove it home from southern Illinois to Northville Mi a trip of 550 miles, a good introduction to a new car. Anyway thanks for putting together a great car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Chris McDonald||09-30-2001||06:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mafv8:<BR><B>Chris, I bought your Fiero from Jeff Lorenz back in July and I have been enjoying it ever since, when I bought it I drove it home from southern Illinois to Northville Mi a trip of 550 miles, a good introduction to a new car. Anyway thanks for putting together a great car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That is cool.  I do miss it from time to time, but now have my hands full with the 69 Corvette project.  I really never planned on selling the Fiero after all that work and time.  I had even obtained a fuel injection system for it.  Future plans included tinting the windows, upgrading the stereo, fuel injection, custom steering wheel, racing seats with four point harness, and better gauges.  I also planned on updating the headlight motors to the newer style.  <P>I hope you enjoy it and win plenty of awards.<P> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||submitmls||10-01-2001||12:56 AM|||| I was just wondering WHICH radiator you're going to use. Is there one that fits, i.e., from another car? I have a v8 with the 4cylinder radiator...because I had to lean out the carb to pass emmissions, the car now runs hotter. I have been told that a v-6 radiator is a bolt-in and it should show cooloing improvements over a 4 cyl rad. true?<BR>I know archie sells a $350 4 core radiator but I also have heard that you can fit on it out of a Corvette? But which model and year vette? Thanks for any detailed replies...||||reg||10
Z||000014||LT1 Chop Top||10-01-2001||01:14 AM||||Nice rims!<P>WHERE is that car now??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||JayUSA77||10-01-2001||01:28 AM||||hey nice wheels, where did you get them from and how much, do they sell the outline in black instead of red????<BR>PM me<BR>thanks,<BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||Chris McDonald||10-01-2001||09:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by submitmls:<BR><B> I was just wondering WHICH radiator you're going to use. Is there one that fits, i.e., from another car? I have a v8 with the 4cylinder radiator...because I had to lean out the carb to pass emmissions, the car now runs hotter. I have been told that a v-6 radiator is a bolt-in and it should show cooloing improvements over a 4 cyl rad. true?<BR>I know archie sells a $350 4 core radiator but I also have heard that you can fit on it out of a Corvette? But which model and year vette? Thanks for any detailed replies...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>This was done with an Archie Master Kit that came with a four core radiator.  However, that alone did not make this car cool properly in the Florida heat with the air on.  So, I made some trick modifications that included stiffening and lowering the front lower air scoop.  I made holes in the spare tire carrier on both sides, then built pressure flaps in the stock shroud so air can easily pass through at highway speed, but be efficiently channeled through the shroud when in traffic.  <P>I guess the car is in Michigan now.<P>I don't even know if you can still get those rims.  They had a silver, red, and blue backing available back then.<BR>||||reg||1
